── what the two runs differ on, and why it matters ────────────────────
Signed out the result offers 4 actions [Download | Start over | Open in Editor |
Sign in to save to Workspace]; signed in the fourth becomes [Save to Workspace].
M5 reads the set off the page rather than hard-coding it, so both runs are held
to the same rule and neither can pass by knowing less than the product offers.

M5b — "a disabled action can be enabled from the keyboard" — is NOT EXERCISED in
both runs, and honestly: the result panel offered no disabled action to enable.
Signed in, Save to Workspace is live immediately because a default destination is
already selected. Nothing was faked to reach that gate.

── the first run of this scenario reported 4 failures. All four were its own ──
PRODUCT FAILURE  M2 ... hidden file input received 0 programmatic click(s)
PRODUCT FAILURE  M3 ... attached true, 40 Tabs never reached "Merge PDFs"
NOT EXERCISED    M4, M5, M6, M7 — "the action was never reached by Tab"

Cause, measured with a standalone diagnostic (/tmp/kbdiag.mjs) rather than
reasoned about: `tabThrough(n)` reports n stops and LEAVES focus on the nth, not
on the one the caller was looking for. The drop control is stop 13 of a 24-stop
sweep, so the Enter went to stop 24 — a footer link, which Enter obediently
followed. The page then under test was /tools, which has no "Merge PDFs" button,
so M3 and everything after it failed for a reason that had nothing to do with the
product. The diagnostic on the same commit, with focus placed on the control:

  focused before Enter: {"active":"DIV/button","name":"Drop your PDFs here. …"}
  pickers after Enter at the dropzone: 1
  pickers after Space:                 2          (both keys are bound)
  staged: {"pdfs":2,"btn":{"text":"Merge PDFs","disabled":false,"tabIndex":0}}
  tab 16  button "Merge PDFs"      ← reachable all along; the strict
  strict /^merge pdfs?$/i match at tab 16    regex was never the problem

Fixed in the probe, not in the product: M2 and M4 now re-tab to the exact stop
they found (`tabThrough(dropStop + 1)`) and assert which control the key reached,
so a mis-focused Enter reports itself as a probe fault instead of a defect. Focus
is placed by Tab and never by `element.focus()` — except at M6, which says so.
Classified PROBE DEFECT: the product was correct on every one of the four rows.

── keyboard-only, end to end ──────────────────────────────────────────
Zero Input.dispatchMouseEvent calls occur in scenario M. The recorded activation
trail is the proof: ["INPUT","Merge PDFs","Start over"] — the hidden file input
clicked by the dropzone's Enter handler, the tool's own action, and the result
action, in that order, all from Tab and Enter.
